WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.369
Hi, friends. Gani here. This is Facial Feelings Detective.

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:16.738
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:16.738 --> 00:00:19.359
Hands resting on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:21.980
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:29.844
Detectives look for clues. A face gives clues about feelings.

00:00:29.844 --> 00:00:37.708
But hear this part: a clue is a hint. A clue is never the whole story.

00:00:37.708 --> 00:00:42.950
A face can hint how someone feels. It can never tell for sure.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.445
So detectives guess gently, with "might be." Let's practice.

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:52.562
Look at this face: eyebrows up, mouth in a small smile.

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:58.678
This person might be feeling okay. Might be. We can't know yet.

00:00:58.678 --> 00:01:04.794
This face: eyebrows squeezed, mouth turned down.

00:01:04.794 --> 00:01:11.784
This person might be having a hard moment. Or might be thinking hard. Both fit the clue.

00:01:11.784 --> 00:01:16.153
And this quiet face? It gives almost no clue at all. Quiet faces are allowed to be quiet.

00:01:16.153 --> 00:01:24.017
So what does a detective do with a clue? We never announce what someone feels.

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:30.133
We check. We ask, soft and kind: "Are you okay?"

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:37.123
And then — the most important part — we believe the answer.

00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:44.113
If they say "I'm fine," we believe them, even if the clue looked different. Their words win.

00:01:44.113 --> 00:01:50.230
Faces hint. People tell. Detectives ask and believe.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:57.220
And if a feeling ever feels too big — yours or a friend's — tell a grown-up. Always.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.841
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:01:59.841 --> 00:02:03.336
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:03.336 --> 00:02:06.831
You're ready for what's next.
